If you dig back through Google around July 5th or so, you can find my
original announcement, but here is a shortish version:

I've played Nethack for a long time.  I like it, but I (like others)
feel that after the Castle, the game largely turns into a boring slog.
All the problems you'll face have well-defined binary solutions, and the
resources the player has are typically far more than sufficient to deal
with any problems he may encounter.  In addition, certain resources and
a certain style of play allow one to effectively bypass huge portions of
the game completely, with no real downside.

Spork is an effort to rebalance the game entirely; to make it
challenging even for experienced players, and make the post-Castle game
actually matter.  It's also an effort to do so while staying relatively
'inside' the Nethack mythos; in other words, it doesn't want to become
Crawl nor Slash'EM, and it definitely doesn't want to become nastier for
new/inexperienced/unspoiled players.

It's important to differentiate between 'challenging' and 'harder' here; 
the best example I know of is One-Eyed Sam in Slash'EM, who is 
definitely 'harder', but not much more challenging since you can just
ignore him if you don't have the solution for him.

There's a full change list on the webpage (see signature); at the moment
I'm holding off any changes that would break save compatibility for at
least the next few weeks, to give people time to really sit down and
play without being worried that their game will suddenly vanish from
under them.

